Moth has one closed family of numeric types plus a nominal octet type.
That family is `Int`, `Uint`, `Float`, the fixed-width integers and binary
floats, and the `Dec` family.

### `Int`, `Uint` and `Float`

`Int` is Moth's ordinary signed integer type. `Uint` is Moth's ordinary
unsigned integer type. `Float` is Moth's ordinary finite
binary float type.

They are distinct semantic identities. `Int` is not an alias for `I32` or
`I64`, `Uint` is not an alias for `U32` or `U64`, and `Float` is not an
alias for `F32` or `F64`, even when the selected
profile gives them the same physical precision.

One compiler-owned numeric profile selects the `Int` and `Float` widths independently.
`Uint` follows the selected `Int` width. There is no separate `Uint` setting.

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    Int width    Float precision    Uint width and range
    32-bit       32-bit             Uint32, 0 through 4294967295
    32-bit       64-bit             Uint32, 0 through 4294967295          (default)
    64-bit       32-bit             Uint64, 0 through 18446744073709551615
    64-bit       64-bit             Uint64, 0 through 18446744073709551615
]

An `Int` value has the range of its selected width.

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    Int width    Range
    32-bit       -2147483648 through 2147483647
    64-bit       -9223372036854775808 through 9223372036854775807
]

All four combinations are valid. The builder supplies the profile before
command input materialises and before configuration compiles. No source,
directive or command line syntax selects it.

The profile is fixed for a whole compilation. It applies to directly linked
Moth modules, generated functions, Core and Builder source packages and
Moth-source dependencies. It never varies by function, target partition or
debug and release representation. Source dependencies compile for that profile.
An incompatible precompiled Moth artefact is rebuilt or rejected, never
silently adapted. Foreign components remain separate value boundaries with
explicit foreign types.

A profile can change `Int` and `Uint` overflow limits, `Float` rounding and folded results.
The same profile preserves behaviour across JavaScript and Wasm.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count Uint = 1 + 2
    large Uint = 3_000_000_000 + 1
]

`large` is valid in both integer profiles. Unannotated `1 + 2` remains
`Int`. See @./#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) for the `Uint`
receiving and peer contract, and @./#operators (Operators) for the
`Uint` operator and comparison rules.

### Fixed-width numeric types

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    signed integers:    I8, I16, I32, I64
    unsigned integers:  U8, U16, U32, U64
    binary floats:      F16, F32, F64
]

Each fixed integer type names its width and signedness exactly.

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    Type   Range
    I8     -128 through 127
    I16    -32768 through 32767
    I32    -2147483648 through 2147483647
    I64    -9223372036854775808 through 9223372036854775807
    U8     0 through 255
    U16    0 through 65535
    U32    0 through 4294967295
    U64    0 through 18446744073709551615
]

`F16`, `F32` and `F64` are IEEE-754 binary16, binary32 and binary64 values.

`Float`, `F16`, `F32` and `F64` are finite. `NaN`, positive infinity and
negative infinity are not valid Moth values. A boundary that could introduce a
non-finite result rejects it.

Fixed integers can promote with other fixed integers inside operators; fixed
binary floats can promote with other fixed binary floats. Neither family
implicitly mixes with `Int` or `Float`, and fixed integers do not implicitly
mix with fixed binary floats. See Promotion and @./#operators (Operators).

### `Byte`

`Byte` is a one-byte octet value. It is nominally distinct from `U8`, `I8` and
`Char`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    raw Byte = 255
]

- `Byte` values support equality and unsigned-octet ordering.
- A contextual whole-number literal from `0` through `255` initialises a `Byte`. A negative value cannot.
- `Byte` takes no part in arithmetic, unary minus or numeric promotion, and it has no range-loop domain.
- Explicit `Byte` and `U8` conversion is infallible in both directions. Other numeric `Byte` conversions compose through `U8`.
- `{Byte}` remains an ordinary collection. Compact physical storage does not create a buffer source type or a binary text encoding.

### `Dec` and `DecN`

`Dec` is an arbitrary-precision integer at scale zero. `Dec0` is the
accepted spelling alias for that same scale-zero identity. `Dec1` through
`Dec256` are arbitrary-precision scaled integers representing
`coefficient / 10^scale`.

Scale is part of the semantic identity. `Dec2` and `Dec4` are different
types, even for a value with no fractional digits. Leading-zero scales such as
`Dec01` and scales above 256 are rejected.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    large Dec = 1000000000000000000000
    money Dec2 = 12.34
]

- Whole literals materialise exactly at any scale.
- A decimal or exponent literal must be exactly representable at the receiving scale, including exact integrality at scale zero. `money Dec2 = 1.239` is invalid rather than rounded.
- Integer-to-`Dec` conversion and scale widening are exact and infallible. Scale narrowing and `Dec`-to-integer conversion are exact or fail. `Uint` joins the exact mixed-integer category with its full unsigned value, including values above `Int` maximum.
- Binary floats and `Byte` never participate in `Dec` arithmetic implicitly.
- Canonical `Dec` text trims fractional trailing zeroes, never uses exponent notation or negative zero and always writes a leading zero before a fractional value.
- A `Dec` receiver types unresolved literal arithmetic before operator selection. An explicit scale reaches through arithmetic and parentheses, and a typed `Dec` peer supplies its scale to a literal-only opposite operand from either side. Only `Dec` and `Uint` peers extend to compound literal subtrees, with different exponent rules: `Dec` keeps an `Int` exponent while a `Uint` exponent stays `Uint`. See @./#numeric-literals (Numeric literals) for the full contract.
- `DecN ^ Int` keeps a non-negative `Int` exponent. A typed `Uint` exponent needs an explicit handled cast to `Int`.

`Dec` is an immutable value. It is not a source-visible `BigInt` and it adds
no numeric trait or overload system.

### Promotion

`Int` may promote to `Float` at a contextual numeric boundary.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count Int = 3
    ratio Float = count
]

Mixed `Int` and `Float` arithmetic produces `Float`. The conversion rounds at
the selected `Float` precision.

`Uint` may promote to `Float` at the same value-receiving and mixed-operator
boundaries. The conversion is direct at the selected `Float` precision and
infallible, although it can lose precision. There is no implicit `Uint`
promotion to or from `Int`, and no implicit `Uint` mixing with fixed-width
values, `Byte`, `Bool` or `Char`.

There is no implicit `Float -> Int` conversion. Use a fallible explicit cast at
an `Int` target.

Typed `Int`, `Uint` and `Float` values do not implicitly mix with fixed-width values.
Fixed integers and fixed binary floats also require an explicit conversion to
mix with each other. Compatible fixed integer `/` operands first select their
common integer type, then divide as `F64`. `Byte` never participates in numeric
promotion.

Widening between fixed integer operands inside an operator is operator
promotion, not a general receiving conversion. It never narrows, and it never
makes an already typed value convertible at an arbitrary receiving boundary. An
implicit receiving boundary never narrows an already typed numeric value.

`Bool` and `Char` are not numeric types.

### Implementation status

This reference owns the accepted contract for the complete numeric family.
@../progress (Progress) owns current compiler and backend support.
The frontend recognises fixed-width types and `Byte`, materialises their literals,
records fixed-width operator facts in HIR, checks their casts and folds numeric
operators, casts and text. HTML-JS executes fixed-width numeric and `Byte` runtime
values, formats `Float` for templates and casts and validates successful external
`Float` returns before ordinary Moth code observes them.

The frontend lazily resolves the scale-zero identity shared by `Dec` and
`Dec0`, plus canonical identities for `Dec1` through `Dec256`. Exact
receiver literals preserve their coefficient and scale through constant
folding, public constants and HIR. HTML-JS transports immutable BigInt-backed
values through ordinary copies, collections, options and generic instances.
Same-scale comparisons stay exact, and templates and `String` share the exact
Dec formatter.

HTML-Wasm executes supported scalar arithmetic (fixed integers, `Byte`, fixed
binary floats, profile-selected `Int`/`Float`/`Uint`) through values, comparisons and
supported infallible numeric expression casts, including integer-to-`F16` and
widening `F16`-to-`F32`/`F64`. Any operation that can actually fail and needs
recoverable delivery is rejected with a structured backend capability
diagnostic; there is no trap fallback. Conservative semantic proof discharges
`Add`, `Subtract`, `Multiply` and `Negate` over fixed-width integer operand
and result domains with full-range containment (for example, `U8 + U8 -> U32`
cannot fail). Other domains are excluded from that discharge; see
@./#checked-arithmetic (Checked arithmetic) for which operations can fail.
The proof is type-domain based and profile-free, identical in debug and release
and across numeric profiles. It also converts profile `Int` and fixed integers
to decimal `String` values.
`Float` formatting and finite-boundary validation are integrity guards, not
implicit numeric failure: they halt unless a declared builtin `Error!` contract
returns them. HTML-Wasm formats runtime `Float` templates and
`Float`/`F16`/`F32`/`F64`-to-`String` expression casts at their own precision,
while negative zero formats as `0`. See @../progress (Progress).

Source-level external `Float` calls still need Wasm import/metadata support.
Reachable `ReturnError` `FormatFloat` and `ValidateFloat`, plus `String`-to-numeric
parsing, remain target-rejected. Wasm rejects reachable `Dec` values and
signatures, including nested types and generated-function uses. Unreachable
private helpers don't gate the target. Other builtin runtime casts stay gated,
including conversions involving `Bool`, `Char` or `Error` (including
`Char -> Int`) and statement-form casts. The frontend implements Dec
arithmetic and exact negation; HTML-JS executes both, with exact mixed
operations and comparisons against profile `Int` and `Uint` and all eight fixed integer
types, rounded half-even per operation. Integer-to-Dec and scale widening
are infallible; scale narrowing and Dec-to-integer conversions are exact
and fallible. HTML-JS `String`-to-`Dec` conversion is fallible and parses
the whole existing numeric grammar exactly; HTML-JS formats Dec canonically.

### MON data boundary

MON literal data consumes the shared numeric facts from `moth-lexical` and keeps
whole-number and decimal or exponent categories lossless until the receiving
schema is known. Standalone Rust callers select a profile through
`moth_mon::Schema::with_profile`, and `Uint` follows the profile `Int` width there too.
The convenience re-export `moth::mon` exposes
the same API and Rust types to compiler clients. `SchemaType::Integer` and
`SchemaType::Decimal` name Rust data families, not additional Moth source types.
Current source syntax uses `Dec` and `DecN`. MON conversion, profile handling
and scale limits belong to @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).
